 for OpenAI\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives a figure (exceeding $100 billion) for NVIDIA's off-balance-sheet guarantees tied to AI-project campuses, the exact quantity the open thesis tracks for disclosure at the next annual report; it is the article's characterization, not the company's reported disclosure.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-7\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article reports that consensus earnings estimates for NVIDIA have been revised sharply higher over just the past nine months, from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"earnings estimates that have been revised sharply higher — from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029 — over just the past nine months.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"consensus EPS estimate (prior)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2029\",\n       \"value\": \"$8.50\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"per share; level before revision over the past nine months\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"consensus EPS estimate (current)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2029\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.68 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"per share; revised sharply higher over the past nine months\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B11\",\n   \"article\": 9,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B12\",\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev1\",\n     \"summary\": \"In May 2026, NVIDIA told shareholders it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share, which the Morningstar author calculates as a 2,400% increase.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"In May, Nvidia told shareholders that it would increase its quarterly payout to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share. That’s a 2,400% increase, according to my calculations!\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": \"quarterly\",\n       \"value\": \"$0.25 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"raised from $0.01 per share\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"dividend increase\",\n       \"period\": \"quarterly\",\n       \"value\": \"2,400%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"author's calculation of the increase from $0.01 to $0.25 per share\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its May 2026 dividend declaration.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Indeed, Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share buyback program\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$80 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"announced alongside the May 2026 dividend declaration\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Morningstar, recounting NVIDIA's 2026 news, cites NVIDIA's role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"AI investments NVIDIA is involved in financing\",\n       \"period\": \"2026\",\n       \"value\": \"$500 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"reported as NVIDIA's role; does not distinguish guarantees, credit support or equity investments\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Indicates the scale of NVIDIA's financial involvement with AI labs and cloud partners, the subject of the credit-support/guarantee thesis; the item does not separate credit support or revenue guarantees from equity investments.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev4\",\n     \"summary\": \"Morningstar, recounting NVIDIA's 2026 news, cites NVIDIA's forecast of 70% year-on-year revenue growth.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"year-on-year\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"NVIDIA forecast cited by Morningstar; the specific period is not stated in the item\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B13\",\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion. According to CNBC, the chipmaker said the increase is the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history and that it expects to complete the remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Shares of leading designer of graphics chips Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) jumped 2.7% in the morning session after the company authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion.\",\n      \"According to CNBC, the chipmaker said the increase is the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history and that it expects to complete the remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"additional share buyback authorization\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$150 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"incremental authorization to the existing share-repurchase program\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total share buyback authorization\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$235 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"total authorization after the increase\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported second-quarter revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year-over-year, and adjusted earnings per share of $2.22, according to a company press release and earnings commentary; both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates of $92.37 billion revenue and $2.09 adjusted EPS. The article says the results were reported about one month before its 28 September 2026 publication.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\",\n      \"According to a company press release and earnings commentary, Nvidia reported revenue of $96.22 billion for the second quarter, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates, which called for revenue of $92.37 billion and adjusted earnings of $2.09 per share.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"about 1 month ago\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company-reported quarterly revenue, per press release and earnings commentary\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"up 106% year-over-year\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"adjusted earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"adjusted (non-GAAP) EPS as reported by the company\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue consensus estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$92.37 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus estimate\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"adjusted earnings per share consensus estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.09 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus estimate\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028), alongside its second-quarter results reported about one month before this 28 September 2026 article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"about 1 month ago\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"next year (fiscal 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company projection for the next year (fiscal 2028)\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B14\",\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B15\",\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported Data Center segment revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, and consolidated revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, per its Aug. 26 press release, as cited by Zacks.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Consolidated revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"consolidated; up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% in fiscal Q2 2027 from 72.5% a year earlier, as cited by Zacks.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; versus 72.5% a year earlier\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which at the midpoint would represent a 12% sequential rise, as cited by Zacks.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company guidance; midpoint implies ~12% sequential rise\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"Zacks states NVIDIA's net profit margin is 63.7%, compared with Sandisk's 56.5%, citing it as evidence of greater efficiency in converting revenue into profit; no period or basis is specified.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"To top it off, NVIDIA's 63.7% net profit margin compared with Sandisk's 56.5%, indicates its greater efficiency in converting revenues into bottom-line profits.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Net profit margin\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"63.7%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"compared with Sandisk's 56.5%; period and basis not stated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B16\",\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B17\",\n   \"article\": 9,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B18\",\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Motley Fool author states that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year (2026), presenting it as evidence of continued growth in the AI build-out.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"fool.com (Motley Fool contributor, author unnamed)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center capital expenditures of the big five AI hyperscalers\",\n       \"period\": \"this year (2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"nearly $800 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Combined spending by the big five AI hyperscalers, as stated by the article's author; no individual company attribution given\",\n       \"party\": \"fool.com (Motley Fool contributor, author unnamed)\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B19\",\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%, and Data Center revenue of $89.023 billion, as recounted in 24/7 Wall St.'s 23 September 2026 article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"105.85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; beat consensus by 6.29%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, and the article states that this Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue, citing geopolitical uncertainty.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\",\n      \"Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"NVIDIA's Q3 fiscal 2027 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue on geopolitical uncertainty, i.e. China data-center compute revenue was not assumed to resume in the guided quarter; this bears on whether NVIDIA's current-generation chips can be sold into China by the end of fiscal 2027.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article states NVIDIA's gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q4\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"range; expected to bottom on memory cost inflation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"range; as volumes scale\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. lists what it calls NVIDIA's real risks as supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and days sales outstanding extending to 60 days.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"supply commitments\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$279 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"guarantee obligations (capped)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"capped\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding (DSO)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"described as extending\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"States supply commitments of $279 billion, a reported baseline for the purchase/supply commitment amount the thesis tracks at NVIDIA's next annual report; the figure is the article's, not a citation to a filing.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"States guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, addressing the amount of guarantees/credit support the thesis tracks at NVIDIA's next annual report; the figure is the article's characterisation and not a filing citation.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang told investors that NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%, according to the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Jensen Huang (NVIDIA)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share of demand NVIDIA can supply\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"described as 'pegged at'\",\n       \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B20\",\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported, per its 26 August 2026 press release, that Data Center segment revenue jumped 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, and that consolidated revenue rose 106% year over year and 18% sequentially to $96.2 billion.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Consolidated revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total; up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% in the fiscal second quarter of 2027 from 72.5% a year earlier, according to the company's 26 August 2026 press release.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; versus 72.5% in the year-ago quarter\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%; at the midpoint that would be a 12% sequential increase, the company's 26 August 2026 guidance as reported by Zacks.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company guidance; midpoint implies a 12% sequential rise per the article\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B21\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027 out of $96 billion in total revenue, and management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, a midpoint the Trefis article calculates as about 12% above the Q2 total.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA said memory prices rose more than it expected and it reset its margin expectations, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in Q2, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations.\",\n      \"NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q4\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected bottom\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, and management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028 — a portion of its business resting on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investment in AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"share of NVIDIA's business from AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly a quarter\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives the scale of NVIDIA's equity investment in AI labs (nearly $50 billion) alongside management's expectation that balance-sheet-backed labs reach roughly a quarter of its fiscal 2028 business, informing the thesis on how much credit support and revenue backstop NVIDIA discloses versus equity investments.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"Trefis calculated that NVIDIA's revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for Texas Instruments, and that NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"trefis.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"over the past year\",\n       \"value\": \"83%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"trefis.com\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"operating profit as a share of sales\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"65.2 cents\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"of each sales dollar\",\n       \"party\": \"trefis.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B22\",\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center segment revenue of $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, and consolidated revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"consolidated revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"consolidated; up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% in the fiscal second quarter of 2027 from 72.5% a year earlier.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; versus 72.5% a year earlier\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which at the midpoint would represent a 12% sequential increase.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%; midpoint implies a 12% sequential rise\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B23\",\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA told shareholders in May 2026 that it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share — a 2,400% increase on Morningstar's calculation — which the article estimates returns roughly $25 billion a year to shareholders, enough to make NVIDIA one of the largest dividend payers in the US market.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"In May, Nvidia told shareholders that it would increase its quarterly payout to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share. That’s a 2,400% increase, according to my calculations!\",\n      \"But in absolute terms, Nvidia’s dividend is huge. Something like $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders. That sum will make Nvidia one of the largest dividend payers in the US stock market.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": \"quarterly, from the May 2026 declaration\",\n       \"value\": \"$0.25 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"increased from $0.01 per share; 2,400% increase per the author's calculation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"annual dividend payout to shareholders\",\n       \"period\": \"annual\",\n       \"value\": \"$25 billion per year\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"author's calculation ('something like'), derived from the raised quarterly payout\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its May 2026 dividend declaration; Morningstar notes most of NVIDIA's distributions to shareholders come as share repurchases rather than dividends.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Indeed, Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration.\",\n      \"“Most of Nvidia’s distributions to shareholders come in the form of share repurchases,” writes Colello.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share buyback program\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$80 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"announced at the time of the May 2026 dividend declaration\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has forecast 70% year-on-year revenue growth, cited by Morningstar as one of the major NVIDIA news items of 2026 and as inconsistent with the view that the dividend hike signals a slower-growth phase; the item does not state which period the 70% growth forecast covers.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\",\n      \"Some might see Nvidia’s dividend hike as a sign the company is entering a new, more slow-growth life stage. That doesn’t fit with the 70% revenue growth forecast.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"year-on-year revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"NVIDIA forecast; period not specified in the item\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B24\",\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 that beat consensus by 6.29%, and Data Center revenue of $89.023 billion; management guided Q3 fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion. Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total revenue, up 105.85% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; beat consensus by 6.29%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company guidance (excludes China Data Center compute revenue per a separate statement)\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"The 24/7 Wall St. analyst lists as real risks to the NVIDIA thesis supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and days sales outstanding extending to 60 days.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"supply commitments\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$279 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"as cited by the analyst in the bear-case risk list; disclosure date and scope not stated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"guarantee obligations (capped)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"capped, as cited by the analyst in the bear-case risk list; counterparties not stated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding (DSO)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"DSO extending to, per analyst\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The cited $108.5 billion of capped guarantee obligations is the kind of credit support clean-1 tracks, indicating the current disclosed scale ahead of the next annual report; the item does not break out which partners or how much is new.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The cited $279 billion of supply commitments is a current disclosed scale for the purchase/supply commitment question; the item does not identify the reporting date of the balance or the counterparties.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q4 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected to bottom on memory cost inflation; company margin outlook\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected recovery as volumes scale\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B25\",\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion. According to CNBC, the chipmaker said the increase is the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history and that it expects to complete the remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Shares of leading designer of graphics chips Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) jumped 2.7% in the morning session after the company authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion.\",\n      \"According to CNBC, the chipmaker said the increase is the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history and that it expects to complete the remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"additional share repurchase authorization\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$150 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"share buyback program\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total share repurchase authorization\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$235 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"share buyback program, after the additional authorization\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported second-quarter revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22, according to a company press release and earnings commentary cited by the article. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates of $92.37 billion in revenue and $2.09 in adjusted earnings per share.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"According to a company press release and earnings commentary, Nvidia reported revenue of $96.22 billion for the second quarter, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates, which called for revenue of $92.37 billion and adjusted earnings of $2.09 per share.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"about 1 month ago\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year-over-year\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"year-over-year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"adjusted earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"adjusted\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$92.37 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus estimate\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"adjusted earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.09\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"adjusted; Wall Street consensus estimate\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Alongside its second-quarter results, reported about one month before the 28 September 2026 article, NVIDIA projected 70% revenue growth for the next year, fiscal 2028.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"about 1 month ago\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"next year (fiscal 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B26\",\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Motley Fool column states, without naming a source for the figure, that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers in 'this year' — the calendar year of the column's publication, 2026.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"fool.com (The Motley Fool)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center spending by the big five AI hyperscalers\",\n       \"period\": \"this year\",\n       \"value\": \"nearly $800 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"combined capital expenditure of the big five AI hyperscalers\",\n       \"party\": null\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B27\",\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, 2026 for the quarter ended July 26: revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year from $46.7 billion and up 18% sequentially, exceeding its prior guidance of $91 billion plus or minus 2%; non-GAAP net income rose 118% to $54 billion from $24.8 billion; adjusted EPS rose 120% to $2.22; non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%; operating income rose 124% year-over-year to $63.7 billion; Data Center segment revenue was $89 billion, up 117% year-over-year and the majority of total revenue.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\n      \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\",\n      \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"record; up 106% year-over-year and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Prior revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$91 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company's prior guidance for the quarter, which the result exceeded\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP net income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$54 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; up 118% from $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Adjusted EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"adjusted (non-GAAP); up 120%; exceeded expectations\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; up from 72.5%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 6,\n       \"measure\": \"Operating income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$63.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up 124% year-over-year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 7,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; up 117% year-over-year; majority of total company revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, with the forecast explicitly assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China, per the outlook issued with its Aug. 26, 2026 results.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"GAAP gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's Q3 fiscal 2027 forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China, evidence on the current state of NVIDIA's China data-center business that the thesis asks whether export approval and Chinese acceptance will restore.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e7\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analysts, as reported by barchart.com, forecast NVIDIA EPS growth of 102.4% year-over-year to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by a further 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Street expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Analysts (Street consensus)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$9.25\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analysts' forecast; growth of 102.4% year-over-year\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.33\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analysts' forecast; further growth of 65.7%\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B28\",\n   \"article\": 9,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B29\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue (quarter ended July 26, 2026) of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year — faster than the 85% year-over-year growth of its fiscal first quarter — with the data center business contributing $89 billion of the total and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, as stated in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter (period ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company-reported, fiscal 2027 second quarter ended July 26, 2026\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 first quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment, part of total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$59.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 6,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"126%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's guidance for its fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue, around 12% above fiscal second-quarter revenue, as cited by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Guidance growth versus fiscal Q2 revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"around 12%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"sequential, versus fiscal Q2\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028, and described it as \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\"\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\"\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"described by the CFO as \\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\"\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The CFO's statement that fiscal 2028 growth is limited by manufacturing capacity, not customer demand, bears on how much NVIDIA must commit to purchase and supply capacity for that growth; it does not itself quantify any purchase or supply commitment.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"On the late-August 2026 earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes take effect, a decline from the fiscal second quarter's 75%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin (bottom)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"between 71% and 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin (settling level)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"as price hikes kick in\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"consensus (analysts)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$412 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analysts' consensus\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B30\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion within $96 billion of total revenue for fiscal Q2 2027, and management guided fiscal Q3 2027 total revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2% — a midpoint about 12% above the Q2 total — as reported by Trefis from the company's latest earnings call.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment of NVIDIA's total revenue, as reported\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total revenue, guided range of plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA said memory prices rose more than it expected and that it reset its margin expectations: it guided gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in fiscal Q2, and expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Each company pays for its position. Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations.\",\n      \"NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q4 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"management's expected bottom for gross margin\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, and management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028 — revenue partly resting on customers the company helps fund.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investment in AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"NVIDIA's investment in AI labs, cumulative, as stated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"AI labs' share of NVIDIA's business\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"business from AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet; management expectation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"States the scale of NVIDIA's equity investment in AI labs (nearly $50 billion) and management's expectation that balance-sheet-backed AI labs reach roughly a quarter of its business in fiscal 2028 — directly the investment-versus-support comparison the thesis tracks at the next annual report.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B31\",\n   \"article\": 1,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue up 117% to $89 billion, as reported by barchart.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue surging 117% to $89 billion\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total, more than doubled year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"segment, up 117% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided third-quarter revenue to approximately $108 billion, implying 89% growth, as reported by barchart.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"management has guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, implying 89% growth\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately $108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company revenue, implying 89% growth\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, far above the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled, as reported by barchart.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company revenue growth\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth previously modeled by Wall Street\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"45%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"sell-side consensus expectation before the company's guidance\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"barchart.com reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"barchart.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share of revenue from customers receiving company-backed financing\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 25%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"article's expectation for company revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"company-backed financing funded off NVIDIA's balance sheet\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"potentially $175 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"article's expectation; potential amount, not a reported balance\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives the scale of revenue tied to customers receiving NVIDIA-backed financing (about 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue, potentially $175 billion off its balance sheet), informing the amount of credit support and revenue backstops the thesis tracks at the next annual report.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e6\",\n     \"summary\": \"barchart.com states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduce credit risk not captured by traditional valuation metrics.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": null,\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"off-balance-sheet guarantees\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"exceeding $100 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI; stated as introducing credit risk\",\n       \"party\": null\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Reports a figure of more than $100 billion of off-balance-sheet guarantees tied to an OpenAI-related project, directly bearing on the guarantee and credit-support amount the thesis tracks at the next annual report.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e8\",\n     \"summary\": \"barchart.com reports that analysts' earnings estimates for NVIDIA were revised sharply higher over the past nine months, from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029, alongside 45 of 50 firms rating the stock \\\"Strong Buy\\\".\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"and earnings estimates that have been revised sharply higher — from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029 — over just the past nine months\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Sell-side analyst consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"earnings per share estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2029\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.68 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analyst consensus, revised up from $8.50 over the past nine months\",\n       \"party\": \"Sell-side analyst consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B32\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total revenue for the quarter, as cited by Trefis.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data center segment revenue for the quarter; no accounting basis stated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company total revenue for the quarter; no accounting basis stated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which Trefis calculates is about 12% above the fiscal Q2 total.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company total revenue; management guidance with a ±2% band\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA said memory prices rose more than it expected and it reset its margin expectations, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations.\",\n      \"Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company gross margin for the quarter; no accounting basis stated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company gross margin; management guidance, down from 75% in fiscal Q2\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance (expected bottom)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q4 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company gross margin; management expectation that margin bottoms in fiscal Q4\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, according to Trefis.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investment in AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Cumulative amount described as invested; no period or accounting basis stated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Reports the equity-investment side of the comparison the thesis tracks (guarantees and credit support versus equity investments in AI labs), giving a nearly $50 billion cumulative investment figure, though it does not quantify any guarantee or backstop.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e6\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028, a portion that would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share of NVIDIA's business from AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly a quarter\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Management expectation; NVIDIA-funded AI lab customers as a share of the company's business\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Quantifies how much of NVIDIA's business management expects to come from AI labs it funds, which is the exposure the thesis tracks alongside the credit support, revenue backstops and equity investments NVIDIA discloses.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B33\",\n   \"article\": 9,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B34\",\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"In May 2026 Nvidia told shareholders it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share, an increase of 2,400% by the Morningstar author's calculation. The author estimates that roughly $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders through the dividend, which would make Nvidia one of the largest dividend payers in the US stock market.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"In May, Nvidia told shareholders that it would increase its quarterly payout to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share. That’s a 2,400% increase, according to my calculations!\",\n      \"But in absolute terms, Nvidia’s dividend is huge. Something like $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders. That sum will make Nvidia one of the largest dividend payers in the US stock market.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May 2026 (item: \\\"In May\\\")\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": \"quarterly, declared May 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"$0.25 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"raised from $0.01 per share\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"prior quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": \"before May 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"$0.01 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"annual dividend payout to shareholders\",\n       \"period\": \"per year\",\n       \"value\": \"$25 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Morningstar author's calculation (\\\"Something like\\\")\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its May 2026 dividend declaration, Morningstar reported, adding that most of Nvidia's distributions to shareholders come as share repurchases rather than dividends.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Indeed, Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration. The company’s preference when it comes to cash allocations is clear in the exhibit below.\",\n      \"“Most of Nvidia’s distributions to shareholders come in the form of share repurchases,” writes Colello.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May 2026 (announced at the time of the dividend declaration)\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share buyback program\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$80 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"announced at the time of the dividend declaration\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Morningstar cites Nvidia's own forecast of 70% year-on-year revenue growth, mentioned among the year's news flow and again in noting that the large dividend increase does not fit a claim that Nvidia is entering a slower-growth phase. The item does not state which period the 70% growth forecast covers.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\",\n      \"That doesn’t fit with the 70% revenue growth forecast.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"year-on-year; Nvidia's own forecast, period not specified in the item\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B35\",\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA Corporation reported in its Aug. 26 press release (as cited by Zacks) that for its fiscal second quarter of 2027 Data Center segment revenue was $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, consolidated revenue was $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, and non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5% a year earlier.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release.\",\n      \"Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\",\n      \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenues\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenue growth, year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"117%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Consolidated revenues\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"consolidated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Consolidated revenue growth, year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"consolidated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided revenue for its fiscal third quarter of 2027 to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and said it expects profitability to remain strong; at the midpoint of that guidance revenue would rise 12% sequentially, according to Zacks' account of the company's outlook.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027.\",\n      \"At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenues guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Sequential revenue growth at guidance midpoint\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"12%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"at the midpoint of guidance; sequential\",\n       \"party\": \"Zacks\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B36\",\n   \"article\": 9,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B37\",\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA, in its Aug. 26 press release, reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 consolidated revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, with Data Center segment revenue of $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially; non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5% a year earlier.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\",\n      \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Consolidated revenues\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"consolidated; up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenues\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue growth, year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"117%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment, year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; versus 72.5% a year ago\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenues to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which at the midpoint the article says would be a 12% sequential rise; the company said it further expects profitability to remain strong.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenues (guidance)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Sequential revenue growth at guidance midpoint\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"12%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"sequential rise at the midpoint of guidance; article's calculation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B38\",\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29% and Data Center revenue of $89.023 billion, per 24/7 Wall St.'s buy note.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"105.85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; beat consensus by 6.29%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, according to 24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang told investors the company can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%, as reported by 24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"pegged at approximately 70%\",\n       \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. cites NVIDIA's Vera Rubin production ramp as one of three catalysts for its buy call, saying Vera Rubin lifts platform value per gigawatt to $40 billion from Blackwell's $25 billion and citing management's description of the ramp as the fastest in company history; the item gives no shipment date or volume.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\",\n      \"Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"platform value per gigawatt (Vera Rubin)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$40 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"platform value per gigawatt, versus Blackwell\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"platform value per gigawatt (Blackwell)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$25 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Blackwell comparison basis cited for Vera Rubin\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Reports that the Vera Rubin ramp is under way, with management calling it the fastest in company history, which bears on whether Vera Rubin reaches volume customer shipments in fiscal 2027; the item gives no shipment or revenue-recognition date, so it does not establish the milestone.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e7\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. lists supply commitments of $279 billion among the real risks in the NVIDIA case; the item does not say on what date or in which filing that amount was reported.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"supply commitments\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$279 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"presented as an existing risk; source and as-of date of the figure not stated in the item\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives a figure for NVIDIA's supply commitments ($279 billion) that the thesis tracks against the purchase and supply commitment amount disclosed in the next Form 10-K; the item gives no as-of date, so it cannot be tied to a specific reporting period.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e8\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among the real risks in the NVIDIA case; the item gives no as-of date or source for the figure.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"guarantee obligations (capped)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"capped; source and as-of date not stated in the item\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"States a $108.5 billion cap on NVIDIA's guarantee obligations, the exact quantity the thesis tracks at the next annual report; the item does not say whether this reflects disclosed guarantees, credit support for AI labs and cloud partners, or the analyst's own aggregation.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e9\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. says NVIDIA's days sales outstanding are extending to 60 days and lists this among the real risks in the case.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding (DSO)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"described as extending\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e13\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA expects gross margins to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in fiscal Q4 2027 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale, as reported by 24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q4 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected to bottom, on memory cost inflation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"recovery as volumes scale\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B39\",\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-q2-fy27-results\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%; Data Center revenue was $89.023 billion.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"105.85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-q3-fy27-guidance-china-exclusion\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided Q3 fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion; the Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue, citing geopolitical uncertainty.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\",\n      \"Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"excludes China Data Center compute revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue, showing China data-center sales are not in the Q3 outlook and bearing on whether NVIDIA can resume China sales in FY2027.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-gross-margin-guidance\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71%–72% in Q4 fiscal 2027 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72%–73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q4\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"on memory cost inflation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"as volumes scale\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-supply-guarantees-dso\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. lists NVIDIA's risk exposures as supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and days sales outstanding extending to 60 days.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"supply commitments\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$279 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"guarantee obligations\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"capped at\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding (DSO)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"extending to\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Reports $279 billion of supply commitments, directly the purchase/supply commitment amount clean-2 tracks at the next annual report.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Reports guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, directly the credit-support/guarantee amount clean-1 tracks at the next annual report.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-jensen-supply-demand-growth\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand and pegged fiscal 2028 growth at approximately 70%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Jensen Huang\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share of demand NVIDIA can supply\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"fiscal 2028 growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The CEO's statement that NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand and his ~70% fiscal 2028 growth forecast indicate demand exceeds supply, a demand signal for the Vera Rubin ramp; it does not confirm shipment timing.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-vera-rubin-platform-value\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. says Vera Rubin raises NVIDIA's platform value per gigawatt to $40 billion from Blackwell's $25 billion.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"platform value per gigawatt (Blackwell)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$25 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Blackwell\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"platform value per gigawatt (Vera Rubin)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$40 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Vera Rubin\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Provides an estimate of Vera Rubin's platform value per gigawatt versus Blackwell, informing the economics of the Vera Rubin ramp tracked by the thesis; it does not establish shipment timing.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B40\",\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%; Data Center revenue was $89.023 billion, as cited in the 24/7 Wall St. article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total, up 105.85% year over year; non-GAAP EPS $2.22\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; beat consensus by 6.29%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, and the guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue because of geopolitical uncertainty, as reported in the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\",\n      \"Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company outlook; excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's Q3 revenue guidance is stated to exclude China Data Center compute revenue because of geopolitical uncertainty, which bears directly on whether China data-center revenue can resume in fiscal 2027.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA expects gross margins to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in fiscal Q4 2027 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale, according to the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q4 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected to bottom on memory cost inflation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"recovery as volumes scale\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article cites NVIDIA's risk exposures as supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and days sales outstanding extending to 60 days.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Supply commitments\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$279 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"as cited in the article as a risk exposure\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Guarantee obligations\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"described as capped, as cited in the article\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Days sales outstanding (DSO)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"described as extending to 60 days\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives a stated amount of $108.5 billion of guarantee obligations (described as capped), the kind of credit-support figure clean-1 tracks for disclosure at the next annual report; the article does not say whether it is a disclosed balance or an estimate.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives a stated $279 billion of supply commitments, the purchase/supply obligation quantity clean-2 tracks at the next annual report; the article gives no comparison period or disclosure source.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B41\",\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported second-quarter revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year-over-year, and adjusted earnings per share of $2.22; both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates of $92.37 billion in revenue and $2.09 in adjusted earnings per share, according to the company's press release and earnings commentary.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"According to a company press release and earnings commentary, Nvidia reported revenue of $96.22 billion for the second quarter, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates, which called for revenue of $92.37 billion and adjusted earnings of $2.09 per share.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Second quarter (fiscal 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Quarter, up 106% year-over-year\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year-over-year\",\n       \"period\": \"Second quarter (fiscal 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Year-over-year\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Adjusted earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"Second quarter (fiscal 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Adjusted\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue consensus estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"Second quarter (fiscal 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"$92.37 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Adjusted earnings per share consensus estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"Second quarter (fiscal 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.09\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia projected 70% revenue growth for its next fiscal year (fiscal 2028), according to the article's account of the company's second-quarter report, which it dates to about one month before the 28 September 2026 article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"about 1 month ago\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B42\",\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 results for the quarter ended July 26, 2026, on Aug. 26, 2026: revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year from $46.7 billion and 18% sequentially, exceeding prior guidance of $91 billion plus or minus 2%; non-GAAP net income was $54 billion, up 118% from $24.8 billion; adjusted EPS was $2.22, up 120% and above expectations; non-GAAP gross margin was 75% versus 72.5%; operating income was $63.7 billion, up 124% year over year; and Data Center segment revenue was $89 billion, up 117% year over year and the majority of total company revenue.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\n      \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\",\n      \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"record; up 106% year over year from $46.7 billion and 18% sequentially; exceeded prior guidance of $91 billion plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Prior revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$91 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%; actual revenue exceeded it\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP net income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$54 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up 118% from $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Adjusted EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; up 120%; exceeding expectations\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"versus 72.5% in Q2 fiscal 2026\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 6,\n       \"measure\": \"Operating income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$63.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up 124% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 7,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up 117% year over year; majority of total company revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points; the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%; assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"GAAP and non-GAAP; plus or minus 50 basis points\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The guidance explicitly assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China, setting the baseline for the China market-access question: any resumption of China data-center revenue would be upside to this outlook.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Street expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% year over year to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Street expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Analysts/Street consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$9.25\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analyst consensus; growth of 102.4% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"Analysts/Street consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.33\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analyst consensus; growth of 65.7% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"Analysts/Street consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B43\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (quarter ended 26 July 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year earlier and faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth, with $89 billion of the total from the data center business and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, as reported by fool.com's Daniel Sparks.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up over a year earlier\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal first quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"year-over-year growth\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center business share of total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"net income\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$59.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 6,\n       \"measure\": \"net income growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"126%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"rose year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, about 12% above fiscal second-quarter revenue, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028, an outlook she called \\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\"; fool.com characterised the constraint as manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028 (the year ending in January 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"described by the CFO as “a supply-constrained outlook”\",\n       \"party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management describes the fiscal 2028 revenue outlook as supply-constrained rather than demand-constrained, a premise bearing on how large NVIDIA's purchase and supply commitments to secure capacity will be at the next annual report; the item gives no commitment amount.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes take effect, a small drop from the 75% gross margin reported for the fiscal second quarter, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"between 71% and 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"gross margins should bottom at this level\",\n       \"party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"settle at this level as price hikes kick in\",\n       \"party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analysts' consensus already has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion, according to fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"analysts' consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$412 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B44\",\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 results (quarter ended July 26, 2026, reported Aug. 26): revenue of a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year from $46.7 billion and up 18% sequentially, exceeding prior guidance of $91 billion plus or minus 2%; non-GAAP net income of $54 billion, up 118% from $24.8 billion; adjusted EPS of $2.22, up 120%; non-GAAP gross margin of 75% versus 72.5%; operating income of $63.7 billion, up 124% year over year; and Data Center segment revenue of $89 billion, up 117% year over year and the majority of total revenue.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\n      \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\",\n      \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"record; GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"$46.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth sequentially\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"18%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP net income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$54 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 6,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP net income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"$24.8 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 7,\n       \"measure\": \"Adjusted EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"adjusted (non-GAAP)\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 8,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; prior-year 72.5%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 9,\n       \"measure\": \"Operating income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$63.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 10,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"segment; up 117% YoY\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points; management said the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%; assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"GAAP and non-GAAP, plus or minus 50 basis points\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's Q3 FY2027 forecast explicitly assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China, establishing the baseline that any restored China chip access would change; it does not indicate whether U.S. or Chinese approval will be given.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-7\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analysts' consensus expects NVIDIA EPS growth of 102.4% year over year to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by a further 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Street expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Sell-side analysts (consensus)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$9.25\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"102.4%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.33\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"65.7%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B45\",\n   \"article\": 9,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B46\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion within $96 billion of total revenue in fiscal Q2 2027, and management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, a midpoint the article calculates as about 12% above the Q2 total.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"midpoint about 12% above the fiscal Q2 total\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA said memory prices rose more than it expected and reset its margin expectations: it guided gross margin (sales left after production costs) to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2, and expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Each company pays for its position. Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q4 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected to bottom\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, and management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028 — a portion of the business resting on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investment in AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"invested in AI labs\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"share of NVIDIA's business from NVIDIA-backed AI labs\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly a quarter\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"States the scale of NVIDIA's equity investments in AI labs (nearly $50 billion) and management's expectation that NVIDIA-funded AI labs provide roughly a quarter of its fiscal 2028 business — directly relevant to the thesis's comparison of disclosed guarantees/credit support against equity investments in AI labs and cloud partners.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B47\",\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA told shareholders in May 2026 that it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share, an increase the article's author calculates at 2,400%; the author estimates roughly $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders through the dividend.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"In May, Nvidia told shareholders that it would increase its quarterly payout to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share. That’s a 2,400% increase, according to my calculations!\",\n      \"But in absolute terms, Nvidia’s dividend is huge. Something like $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$0.25 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"prior quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$0.01 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"annual dividend payout\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$25 billion per year\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"author's approximation\",\n       \"party\": \"Morningstar (article author)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its May 2026 dividend declaration.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Indeed, Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share buyback program\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$80 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has forecast 70% year-on-year revenue growth, cited by the article as one of the company's news items in 2026; the item gives no period for the forecast.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"year-on-year revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article cites, among NVIDIA's news this year, its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments; it does not say how much of that is credit support or guarantees rather than equity investment.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"artificial intelligence investments financed with NVIDIA's involvement\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$500 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Morningstar\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Indicates the scale of NVIDIA's financing role in AI investments, the activity whose disclosed guarantees and credit support clean-1 tracks; the item does not break out guarantees or revenue backstops from equity investment, so it speaks only to magnitude.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B48\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total revenue, as cited by Trefis in its comparison with Texas Instruments.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"total company\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, a midpoint about 12% above the fiscal Q2 2027 total.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%; midpoint about 12% above the fiscal Q2 2027 total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal Q3 2027 gross margin to 74%, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company gross margin, share of sales left after production costs\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company gross margin guidance, down from fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance (expected trough)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q4 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company expectation that gross margin bottoms in fiscal Q4 2027\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e6\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, according to Trefis.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investment in AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"cumulative investment in AI labs as of the article; no date given\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives the scale of NVIDIA's equity investment in AI labs, the figure clean-1's outcome contrasts with the credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees NVIDIA discloses at its next annual report.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e7\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028, a part of the business that would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share of business from NVIDIA-backed AI labs\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly a quarter\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"management expectation; share of NVIDIA's business, not a revenue amount\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's expectation of how much of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 business comes from NVIDIA-backed AI labs bears directly on the exposure the guarantees, credit support and equity investments tracked by clean-1 create.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e8\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's revenue grew 83% over the past year and the company kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, per Trefis's comparison with Texas Instruments.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"past year\",\n       \"value\": \"83%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company revenue growth over the past year as computed by Trefis\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"operating margin\",\n       \"period\": \"past year\",\n       \"value\": \"65.2 cents of each sales dollar\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"operating profit as a share of sales, company\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B49\",\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, raising the total authorization to $235 billion, and said it expects to complete the remaining buyback through fiscal 2028; the company described the increase as the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Shares of leading designer of graphics chips Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) jumped 2.7% in the morning session after the company authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion.\",\n      \"According to CNBC, the chipmaker said the increase is the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history and that it expects to complete the remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"additional share buyback authorization\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$150 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total share buyback authorization\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$235 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year-over-year, and adjusted earnings per share of $2.22, according to a company press release and earnings commentary; both topped Wall Street consensus estimates of $92.37 billion revenue and $2.09 adjusted EPS.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\",\n      \"According to a company press release and earnings commentary, Nvidia reported revenue of $96.22 billion for the second quarter, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates, which called for revenue of $92.37 billion and adjusted earnings of $2.09 per share.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"about 1 month ago\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year-over-year\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"adjusted earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"consensus revenue estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$92.37 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Wall Street consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"consensus adjusted earnings per share estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.09\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Wall Street consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia projected 70% revenue growth for the next year, fiscal 2028, in the second-quarter results announcement reported about a month before this article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"about 1 month ago\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"next year (fiscal 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B50\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total revenue, as summarised by Trefis from the company's latest earnings call. Data centers bring in nearly all of NVIDIA's revenue.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"total company\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which Trefis calculates is about 12% above the fiscal Q2 total.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management said memory prices rose more than it expected and that it reset its margin expectations: gross margin was guided to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in fiscal Q2, and is expected to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations.\",\n      \"NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company gross margin\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company gross margin\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin expectation (bottom)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q4\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company gross margin, expected trough\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, and management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028 — business that would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs.\",\n      \"Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investments in AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"invested in AI labs\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"share of NVIDIA's business from NVIDIA-backed AI labs\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly a quarter\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company business, AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives the size of NVIDIA's equity investments in AI labs (nearly $50 billion), the figure the thesis's outcome compares against disclosed guarantees and credit support, and quantifies management's expectation that NVIDIA-funded AI labs reach roughly a quarter of its fiscal 2028 business.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B51\",\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported in its Aug. 26, 2026 press release for the fiscal second quarter of 2027 Data Center segment revenues of $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, and consolidated revenues of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, as summarized by Zacks.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release.\",\n      \"Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenues\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; per the Aug. 26 press release\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenue growth, year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"117%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"consolidated revenues\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"consolidated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"consolidated revenue growth, year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"consolidated; year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% in the fiscal second quarter of 2027 from 72.5% a year earlier, as reported in the company's Aug. 26, 2026 release and summarized by Zacks.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"72.5%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; year-ago quarter\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided revenues for the fiscal third quarter of 2027 to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and Zacks calculated that the midpoint implies a 12% sequential rise; the guidance was reported with the company's Aug. 26, 2026 results.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027.\",\n      \"At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenues (guidance)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"sequential revenue growth at guidance midpoint\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"12%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"calculated at the guidance midpoint\",\n       \"party\": \"Zacks\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B52\",\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B53\",\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia told shareholders in May that it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share, an increase the Morningstar author calculates at 2,400%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"In May, Nvidia told shareholders that it would increase its quarterly payout to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share. That’s a 2,400% increase, according to my calculations!\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": \"quarterly\",\n       \"value\": \"$0.25 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"prior quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": \"quarterly\",\n       \"value\": \"$0.01 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"increase in quarterly dividend\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"2,400%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"author's calculation\",\n       \"party\": \"Morningstar\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration, according to the Morningstar article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Indeed, Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share buyback program\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$80 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Morningstar article refers to Nvidia's own forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth, a rate the author says is inconsistent with the idea that the company is entering a slow-growth stage; the item does not state which period the 70% growth forecast covers.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\",\n      \"That doesn’t fit with the 70% revenue growth forecast.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"year-on-year revenue growth forecast\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Morningstar article describes Nvidia's role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments as one of the major Nvidia news items of the year; it does not break out how much of that is guarantees, credit support or direct equity investment.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"artificial intelligence investments Nvidia has a role in financing\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$500 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"as described by the article; no split between guarantees, credit support and equity investment\",\n       \"party\": null\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Indicates the scale of Nvidia's financial involvement in AI investments ($500 billion) that the annual-report disclosure of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops would quantify; the article does not separate guarantees or credit support from equity investment, so it speaks to magnitude only.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B54\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (quarter ended July 26, 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year — faster than the 85% growth reported in fiscal Q1 — with the data center business contributing $89 billion of the total and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year. The figures were reported in NVIDIA's second-quarter earnings release summarized by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company total, as reported in the second-quarter earnings release\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company total, versus a year earlier\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 first quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company total, versus a year earlier\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data center segment, share of total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$59.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company total, as reported; up 126% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, about 12% above fiscal Q2 2027 revenue, according to the company's quarterly outlook cited in the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company outlook, described as about 12% above fiscal Q2 revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), describing it as \\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\" — with manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand the limiting factor, according to the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth expectation\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company outlook given by CFO on the earnings call; described as supply-constrained\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's statement that fiscal 2028 growth is limited by manufacturing capacity, not demand, bears on whether NVIDIA is taking on larger purchase and supply commitments; the statement itself discloses no commitment amount.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in fiscal Q4 2027 and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect — a decline from the 75% gross margin reported in fiscal Q2 2027.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"between 71% and 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company outlook given by CFO; expected trough\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company outlook given by CFO; after price increases take effect\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Reported quarterly gross margin, as cited in the article\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"According to the article, analysts' consensus already puts NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Analyst consensus (as cited by fool.com)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue consensus\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 (year ending January 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"around $412 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Analyst consensus, as stated in the article\",\n       \"party\": \"Analyst consensus (as cited by fool.com)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B55\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (ended 26 July 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year earlier and faster than the 85% year-over-year growth of the fiscal first quarter; the data center business accounted for $89 billion of the total and net income was $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended 26 July 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company total; up 106% over a year earlier\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended 26 July 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data center business, part of total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended 26 July 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$59.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company net income; up 126% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, about 12% above the fiscal second quarter.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company guidance; around 12% above fiscal Q2\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), calling it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\" in which manufacturing capacity, not customer demand, is the limit.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company expectation stated by CFO Colette Kress; described as a supply-constrained outlook\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The CFO's statement that growth is limited by manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand implies the company is securing more supply from manufacturing partners, which bears on the purchase and supply commitment amount that clean-2 tracks; the item gives no commitment figure and this does not establish any specific commitment.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"On the same late-August 2026 earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect, down from 75% in the fiscal second quarter.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"between 71% and 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Expected bottom, stated by CFO Colette Kress\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Expected level as price hikes kick in, stated by CFO Colette Kress\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company gross margin for the quarter\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion, according to the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Analysts' consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue (analysts' consensus)\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$412 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Analysts' consensus\",\n       \"party\": \"Analysts' consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B56\",\n   \"article\": 1,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue up 117% year over year to $89 billion, according to the article's recounting of reported results.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue surging 117% to $89 billion\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company total, more than doubled year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data center segment, up 117% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal third-quarter revenue to approximately $108 billion, which the article says implies 89% growth.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"management has guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, implying 89% growth\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately $108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company guidance, implying 89% growth\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, well above the 45% growth Wall Street had previously modeled.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company guidance from the CFO\",\n       \"party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth previously modeled by Wall Street\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"45%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Prior sell-side consensus model cited by the article\",\n       \"party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article estimates NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"barchart.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Share of revenue from customers receiving company-backed financing\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 25%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Article estimate; 'expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing'\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Company-backed financing funded off NVIDIA's balance sheet\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"potentially $175 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Article estimate of potential exposure\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The article's estimate that roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue comes from customers receiving NVIDIA-backed financing, with potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's balance sheet, bears directly on the scale of credit support and revenue backstops the thesis asks NVIDIA to disclose at its next annual report. It is an article estimate, not a company disclosure.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-6\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article says NVIDIA carries off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion, tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduce credit risk not captured by traditional valuation metrics.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"barchart.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Off-balance-sheet guarantees\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"exceeding $100 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Article estimate; tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"A claimed >$100 billion of off-balance-sheet guarantees tied to OpenAI-related projects is evidence on the magnitude of credit support the thesis tracks, though it is the article's assertion rather than an NVIDIA disclosure. It does not establish the exact amount NVIDIA will report.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-8\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article reports that consensus earnings estimates for NVIDIA have been revised sharply higher over the past nine months, from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"and earnings estimates that have been revised sharply higher — from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029 — over just the past nine months\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Sell-side analysts (consensus)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Consensus EPS estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2029\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.68 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Consensus estimate after revisions over the past nine months\",\n       \"party\": \"Sell-side analysts (consensus)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B57\",\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 results (quarter ended July 26, 2026, announced Aug. 26) with record revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year from $46.7 billion and up 18% sequentially, comfortably exceeding the company's prior guidance of $91 billion plus or minus 2%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26.\",\n      \"Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially.\",\n      \"The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total record quarterly revenue; up 106% YoY from $46.7 billion and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance (prior guidance for the quarter)\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$91 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"prior company guidance, exceeded by actual revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported non-GAAP profitability for fiscal second-quarter 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026): net income rose 118% to $54 billion from $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, adjusted EPS rose 120% to $2.22 (above expectations), non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%, and operating income rose 124% year over year to $63.7 billion.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations.\",\n      \"Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP net income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$54 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; up 118% from $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Adjusted EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"adjusted (non-GAAP); up 120%, exceeding expectations\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; up from 72.5%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Operating income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$63.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up 124% YoY; GAAP/non-GAAP basis not stated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's Data Center segment generated $89 billion of revenue in fiscal second-quarter 2027, up 117% year over year and representing the majority of total company revenue, reflecting demand from hyperscalers and AI companies expanding infrastructure.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; up 117% YoY, majority of total company revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, with the forecast explicitly assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China, and guided GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\n      \"Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company outlook; assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"GAAP and non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The guidance's stated assumption of no Data Center compute revenue from China describes the current state of NVIDIA's China business and informs the question of whether China data-center revenue can resume under export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e8\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Street expects NVIDIA's EPS growth to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% year over year to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by a further 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Street expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Street analysts (consensus)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$9.25\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analyst consensus; implies 102.4% YoY growth\",\n       \"party\": \"Street analysts (consensus)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.33\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analyst consensus; implies 65.7% YoY growth\",\n       \"party\": \"Street analysts (consensus)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B58\",\n   \"article\": 1,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter revenue of $96.2 billion, more than double the year-ago quarter, with data center revenue up 117% year over year to $89 billion, as reported by barchart.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue surging 117% to $89 billion, and management has guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, implying 89% growth.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total; more than doubled year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment; up 117% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided for approximately $108 billion of third-quarter revenue, which the article says implies 89% growth.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"management has guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, implying 89% growth.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"approximately; implies 89% growth\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, a figure far above the 45% growth Wall Street had previously modeled.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company guidance\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth previously modeled by Wall Street\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"45%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"prior consensus model, as stated by the article\",\n       \"party\": \"Wall Street\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"According to barchart.com, NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"barchart.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Share of revenue from customers receiving company-backed financing\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 25%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected; customers receiving some form of NVIDIA-backed financing\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$175 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"potentially; tied to the share of fiscal 2028 revenue from financed customers\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The article's estimate that about a quarter of fiscal 2028 revenue, potentially $175 billion, comes from customers receiving NVIDIA-backed financing bears directly on the thesis's question of how much credit support and revenue backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e6\",\n     \"summary\": \"barchart.com reports that NVIDIA carries off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, creating credit risk.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"barchart.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Off-balance-sheet guarantees\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$100 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"exceeding $100 billion; tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"A reported figure of more than $100 billion in off-balance-sheet guarantees tied to named AI projects is direct evidence on the amount of credit support and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended, the quantity the thesis tracks.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e7\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analysts' earnings per share estimates for NVIDIA have been revised sharply higher over the past nine months, from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029, according to barchart.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts remain overwhelmingly bullish, with 45 of 50 firms rating the stock a \\\"Strong Buy,\\\" a mean price target near $326 implying 42% upside from current levels, and earnings estimates that have been revised sharply higher — from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029 — over just the past nine months.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Earnings per share estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2029\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.68 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"current analyst estimate, revised up over the past nine months\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B59\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (period ended July 26, 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year — faster than the 85% year-over-year growth of its fiscal first quarter — with the data center business accounting for $89 billion of the total and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, per the company's second-quarter earnings release as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter (period ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center business, of company total\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$59.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, around 12% above its fiscal second-quarter revenue, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"around 12% above fiscal Q2\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"On Nvidia's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), describing it as \\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\" in which the limit is manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"\\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\"\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The CFO's statement that the fiscal 2028 outlook is constrained by manufacturing capacity rather than demand bears on whether Nvidia must lock in large purchase and supply commitments; the item gives no amount of such commitments, so it does not establish their size.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"On Nvidia's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes take effect, down from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"between 71% and 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"bottom level\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"steady level as price hikes kick in\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"According to analyst consensus cited by fool.com, Nvidia's fiscal 2027 revenue is expected to come in around $412 billion, implying roughly 90% revenue growth for the fiscal year; the article uses those consensus figures as the base for its valuation arithmetic.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion.\",\n      \"Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Analyst consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"around $412 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analysts' consensus\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"this fiscal year (fiscal 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"90%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected growth\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B60\",\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, and non-GAAP EPS of $2.22, beating consensus by 6.29%; Data Center revenue was $89.023 billion.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"105.85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided Q3 fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion; the article states the Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue, citing geopolitical uncertainty.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\",\n      \"Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"excludes China Data Center compute revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The exclusion of China Data Center compute revenue from Q3 guidance indicates China data-center revenue had not resumed and bears on whether U.S. and Chinese restrictions allow sales by the end of fiscal 2027.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article states NVIDIA's gross margins are expected to bottom at 71% to 72% in Q4 fiscal 2027 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St. analyst\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q4 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St. analyst\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St. analyst\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. lists NVIDIA's real risks as supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and days sales outstanding extending to 60 days.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Supply commitments\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$279 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Guarantee obligations\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"capped at\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Days sales outstanding (DSO)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"States NVIDIA's supply commitments at $279 billion, directly bearing on the thesis's tracked purchase and supply commitment amount.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"States NVIDIA's guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, directly bearing on the thesis's tracked guarantee and credit-support amount.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e8\",\n     \"summary\": \"Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA / Jensen Huang\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Fiscal 2028 growth\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA / Jensen Huang\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's statement that NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand and its fiscal 2028 growth outlook indicate a supply-constrained demand backdrop for the Vera Rubin ramp, though it does not establish shipment timing.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; d=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1.json\\\")); print(len(d[\\\"articles\\\"]),len(d[\\\"answers\\\"])); [(print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"NAMED\\\",a.get(\\\"named_figures\\\"),\\\"QUESTIONS\\\",a.get(\\\"open_questions\\\")),print(a[\\\"text\\\"])) for a in d[\\\"articles\\\"][3:]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; d=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1.json\\\")); print(len(d[\\\"articles\\\"]),len(d[\\\"answers\\\"])); [(print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"NAMED\\\",a.get(\\\"named_figures\\\"),\\\"QUESTIONS\\\",a.get(\\\"open_questions\\\")),print(a[\\\"text\\\"])) for a in d[\\\"articles\\\"][3:]]'\"","aggregated_output":"python3: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\npython3: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-6g5WIXZ1' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-11-14 19:13:14.431 xcodebuild[57179:49176699]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-11-14 19:13:14.546 xcodebuild[57179:49176686] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\npython3: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\npython3: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-ahz3yPwV' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-11-14 19:13:14.879 xcodebuild[57181:49176728]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-11-14 19:13:14.988 xcodebuild[57181:49176727] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\n10 60\nARTICLE 4 Dear Nvidia Stock Fans, Mark Your Calendars for September 24 NAMED [] QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}]\nNvidia (NVDA) investors have a key date to watch as CEO Jensen Huang is set to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, along with other prominent technology leaders at the White House. The high-profile gathering comes as the U.S. and China navigate sensitive issues involving artificial intelligence (AI), trade, tariffs, and advanced semiconductor technology.\nHuang's presence is particularly notable for Nvidia, given the company's position at the center of the global AI chip market and the ongoing U.S.-China technology relationship. Investors will be watching whether discussions around AI and semiconductor trade create any signals relevant to Nvidia's access to the Chinese market, export restrictions, or the broader outlook for AI infrastructure demand.\nIntel vs. TSMC: One Chip Stock Controls 72.5% of the Foundry Market. The Other Is Mounting a Comeback.\nWhile the dinner itself does not guarantee any policy changes, Sept. 24 could provide an important backdrop for Nvidia stock as the market weighs the potential implications of renewed U.S.-China engagement.\nNvidia is a global leader in accelerated computing and AI, renowned for pioneering the GPU that revolutionized gaming, data centers, and AI-driven computing. Headquartered in Santa Clara, California, Nvidia's technology now powers everything from high-performance gaming and cloud computing to autonomous vehicles and generative AI applications. With a market cap of $5.5 trillion, Nvidia stands among the world's most valuable companies, driven by its dominance in AI infrastructure and continued innovation in next-generation chip design.\nNvidia stock has gained steady momentum in recent sessions, with the shares now sitting just 4% below their 52-week high of $236.54 reached on May 14. The stock is up 22% year-to-date (YTD) and 28% over the past 52 weeks, while its five-day gain stands at 7%, reflecting an acceleration in recent trading. Although in the red this morning, Nvidia has otherwise advanced in each of the past five sessions through Sept. 22, including gains of 2.5% on Sept. 17, 1.3% on Sept. 18, 2.3% on Sept. 21, and 0.66% yesterday, Sept. 22. It surged marginally in the last session, closing at $228.87.\nNvidia's recent stock advance has been supported by several developments reinforcing the strength of the AI infrastructure cycle. The company's latest quarterly results showed revenue growth of 106%, while management has continued to signal strong demand for its next-generation AI systems.\nMeanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong. The stock is also benefiting from renewed optimism around the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin platforms as customers continue expanding AI infrastructure.\nIn addition, expectations surrounding the upcoming Trump-Xi engagement have added another potential catalyst, with investors watching for any developments that could improve Nvidia's ability to sell AI chips in China.\nNVDA trades at 24.04 times forward earnings and 24.81 times sales, which is currently a premium compared to industry peers.\nNvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\nThe company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\nNvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\nFurthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\nIt's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\nStreet expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\nBernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon reiterated a \"Buy\" rating on NVDA stock this week while maintaining a $400 price target. The target was unchanged from Rasgon's earlier revision, when Bernstein raised it from $315 to $400 on Aug. 27, underscoring the analyst's continued confidence in Nvidia's earnings and AI-driven growth outlook.\nFurthermore, Rothschild & Co Redburn analyst Timm Schulze-Melander maintained a \"Buy\" rating on Nvidia with a $325 price target.\nOverall, NVDA stock has a consensus \"Strong Buy\" rating. Of the 50 analysts covering it, 46 advise a \"Strong Buy,\" three suggest a \"Moderate Buy,\" and one offers a \"Strong Sell\" rating.\nThe average analyst price target for NVDA is $325.88, indicating a potential upside of 45%. The Street-high target price of $515 suggests that the stock could rally as much as 129%.\nARTICLE 5 I've Held Nvidia for Nearly 3 Years. Here's Whether I'd Still Buy Today. NAMED [] QUESTIONS None\nNvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has been one of the best stocks to own in the artificial intelligence (AI) arms race. It is up nearly 1,500% since 2023 began, providing investors with incredible returns. Unfortunately, I do not have those returns in my portfolio.\nI bought the stock at the beginning of 2023 and watched it double. Then I sold the stock because I was worried that AI computing demand wouldn't last. I was very wrong. I had to disregard all of the prior gains and purchase shares in 2024 after it had run up some more, which has still been a great investment for me.\nBut would I buy the stock again today? You bet I would.\nNvidia is still a dominant force in the industry\nNvidia is turning the investment world on its head. Normally, the law of large numbers dictates that the larger a company is, the harder it is to grow. Nvidia is the world's largest company and has reached heights that no other company has ever achieved. However, it's growing faster than any other stock among the world's top 10 companies. That's hard to believe, but it showcases just how massive the demand is for AI computing power.\nNvidia makes GPUs , which are the go-to computing unit for all sorts of AI operations, be it training or inference. While there are alternatives, the Nvidia ecosystem cannot be beaten. Additionally, custom AI chips are starting to become more popular among many AI hyperscalers. Still, there will always be a place for computing units like Nvidia's GPUs that are flexible and can handle a wide variety of workloads.\nDue to Nvidia's market position, it has advanced knowledge of how much computing power the AI hyperscalers are planning. The AI companies are making multiyear plans to ensure they have mapped out when they will need computing units, because data centers don't go up overnight. This gives Nvidia the ability to issue long-term guidance, and the guidance regarding the AI build-out is simply incredible.\nNvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030. Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\nSo, if you think you've missed the boat on Nvidia, think again. There is still plenty of upside to come, and Nvidia will be one of the biggest beneficiaries of the AI build-out. However, none of it is priced into Nvidia's stock.\nThat's pretty much in line with where other big tech stocks trade when one-time effects don't skew their valuations, but there's one problem: Nvidia is growing far faster than any of its big tech peers. The trailing price-to-earnings ratio doesn't account for Nvidia's massive growth ahead, and that's where a forward-looking metric helps out.\nAt less than 15 times next year's earnings, Nvidia's stock has almost no growth priced into it today. That means the stock has major upside over the next year. Furthermore, with the AI build-out expected to intensify through 2030, that leaves plenty more room for Nvidia to run.\nEven if you've missed out on Nvidia so far, now is the perfect time to buy more. Very little growth has been priced in, and Nvidia's stock looks well positioned to be one of the best investments over the next few years.\nARTICLE 6 Nvidia Stock Set For 40% Surge: 3 Catalysts That Could Drive the Next Big Move NAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': 'fiscal 2028 growth of approximately 70%'}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand'}] QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}]\nNVDA earns a BUY with a $310.20 target (~40% upside), driven by the Vera Rubin ramp and $500 billion in mobilized AI infrastructure capital.\nAMD's trailing P/E of 211 dwarfs NVDA's 45, while AVGO's AI semiconductor revenue surged 221%, confirming the AI infrastructure wave underpinning NVDA's outlook.\nMy 24/7 Wall St. price target for NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) is $310.20, which implies 39.56% upside from the current $225.35 quote. I rate NVDA a buy with high conviction.\nThree catalysts drive the call: the Vera Rubin production ramp, more than $500 billion in third-party AI infrastructure capital being mobilized through NVIDIA partners, and the opening chapter of physical AI across robotics and autonomous vehicles.\nNVDA is up 21.11% year to date and 28.17% over the trailing year. Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion. Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\nWhy Bulls See $353 or Higher\nThe bull-case one-year target is $353.54. Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%. Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\nHyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027, a wave that also pulls in the power, cooling, and networking suppliers we profiled in a free report on seven AI infrastructure names that aren't chipmakers. Analyst sentiment shows 57 Buy or Strong Buy ratings against 2 Holds and 1 Sell.\nThe bear scenario bottoms at $261.39, still above current price. Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\nQ3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty. Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) posted Q2 fiscal 2026 revenue of $11.536 billion, up 50.11%, with Data Center up 107%. AMD trades at a trailing P/E of 211 versus NVDA's 45, making NVDA the cheaper name on growth per valuation dollar.\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) competes in custom AI ASICs and networking. Q3 fiscal 2026 revenue reached $29.591 billion, up 85.5%, with AI semiconductor revenue of $16.70 billion growing 221% year over year. Its acceleration validates NVDA's guided $108.0 billion quarter sits inside a real industry wave. The peer set makes $310.20 look reasonable.\nI'd own NVDA at $225.35 with a 24/7 Wall St. price target of $310.20 and a buy rating at 90% confidence. Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\nI'd stay a buyer if hyperscaler capex tracks toward 1.3 trillion in 2027. I'd step aside if memory pricing pushes gross margin below 71% or if China restrictions widen beyond the Data Center compute carve-out.\nThese projections assume NVIDIA executes on the Vera Rubin ramp and hyperscaler AI spending holds. Significant upside or downside could result from broader China market access, a major AI capex reset, or accelerated adoption of physical AI and robotics platforms.\nARTICLE 7 What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not? NAMED [] QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}]\nWhat Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not?\nTexas Instruments (TXN) and NVIDIA both sell chips for the data centers now being built. Data centers bring in nearly all of NVIDIA’s revenue, while TI sells to industrial and automotive customers as well as data centers. You pay 38.2 times the yearly profit for TI, yet only 28.1 times for NVIDIA. Their latest earnings calls show the two businesses parting ways on supply.\nNVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\nTI guided Q3 2026 revenue to $5.65 billion to $6.15 billion, against $5.5 billion in Q2. TI’s data center sales doubled from a year earlier in Q2. Management said most of the Q3 growth will come from selling more units, not from higher prices. TI also has clean room space it can equip if demand keeps rising.\nThe two outlooks are different kinds of news. Demand for NVIDIA’s chips is larger than NVIDIA’s supply, so supply sets the pace. TI has spare room to make more, so demand sets TI’s pace.\nEach company pays for its position. Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\nNVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\nTI’s cost is its factories. TI spent $3.3 billion on capital projects in the year to Q2 2026. TI’s $8.7 billion of cash from operations covered that more than twice over. TI’s free cash flow over the same period included $1.6 billion of CHIPS Act incentives. The yearly numbers on growth, margin, and debt show how far apart the two already are.\nThis comparison scores six standard measures of price, growth, profit and debt , and NVIDIA leads Texas Instruments on all six. NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\nDebt equals 0.7% of NVIDIA’s market value, against 6.1% for TI. NVIDIA carries almost no debt against its value.\nTI wins on one measure outside those six: price to sales. You pay 11.9 times the yearly revenue for TI, against 17.9 times for NVIDIA. The six scored measures judge price on profit, and on profit NVIDIA is cheaper.\nTI also has a long dividend record. In September, TI raised the quarterly dividend to $1.52 a share, from $1.42. That was TI’s 23rd straight year of dividend increases.\nWhat Would Count As A Stumble For NVIDIA?\nNVIDIA’s lead is wide enough that it would take a real stumble to shrink it. Revenue below NVIDIA’s own guided range for fiscal Q3 would be the first sign of one. NVIDIA holds its call on that quarter on November 17. A result inside or above the range would leave NVIDIA’s lead in place.\nNVIDIA leads on growth, margins, debt, and price on profit. TI’s strengths are a lower price on sales, spare capacity, and a long dividend record. Revenue below NVIDIA’s guided range in November is the first result that would test that lead.\nHow To Act On TXN?\nARTICLE 8 Nvidia Would Have to Add Almost a Whole Broadcom to Reach $7 Trillion NAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': \"the fiscal second quarter's 75% gross margin\"}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'gross margin of 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028'}] QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}, {'id': 'clean-6', 'question': 'Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?', 'period': \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"}]\nNvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\n\"Now, compute is revenue,\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\nWhen might it happen?\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\nARTICLE 9 China May Let Alibaba Buy New Nvidia Chips, The Information Says NAMED [] QUESTIONS None\n(Bloomberg) -- The Chinese government has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd to purchase Nvidia Corp.'s new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter.\nEllison Pledges $9.2 Billion More in Oracle Shares for Loans\nMcDonald's Selloff Hits 30% as Big Mac Inflation Spurs Pushback\nThe Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor chip, which was released this month, including how many of them and what they would use the product for, the report said, adding that the ministry told some of the firms it intends to approve the purchases.\nNvidia has faced export restrictions from the US as well as curbs from Beijing over the sale of its chips to China, due to a web of bilateral tensions ranging from security to competition on artificial intelligence.\nThe US administration recently lifted restrictions on some Nvidia chips, though the products tend to be older-generation chips that have been sold in small quantities.\nGLP-1 Users Are Furious After Employers Drop Insurance Benefits\nThis $2 Billion Company Dresses HBO's The Pitt Cast and Real Doctors Too\nARTICLE 10 Why Nvidia (NVDA) Stock Is Trading Up Today NAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': 'the $92.37 billion revenue estimate for the quarter'}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'the $96.22 billion revenue of the quarter'}] QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}, {'id': 'clean-6', 'question': 'Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?', 'period': \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"}, {'id': 'clean-7', 'question': \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\", 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.'}]\nShares of leading designer of graphics chips Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) jumped 2.7% in the morning session after the company authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion.\nAccording to CNBC, the chipmaker said the increase is the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history and that it expects to complete the remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028. CEO Jensen Huang said in a statement that Nvidia's growth is being driven by a \"once-in-a-generation platform shift to AI and accelerated computing,\" and that strong cash generation lets the company invest in that buildout while returning capital to shareholders. Speaking on CNBC's \"Squawk Box,\" Huang called the AI infrastructure cycle the largest buildout in human history and said Nvidia plans to return more cash to shareholders as it generates it. A record buyback authorization typically supports the stock by signaling management confidence and reducing share count when cash flow is strong.\nAfter the initial pop, the shares cooled down to $231.14, up 2.7% from the previous close.\nNvidia's shares are not very volatile and have only had 8 moves greater than 5% over the last year. In that context, today's move indicates the market considers this news meaningful, although it might not be something that would fundamentally change its perception of the business.\nThe biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\nAccording to a company press release and earnings commentary, Nvidia reported revenue of $96.22 billion for the second quarter, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates, which called for revenue of $92.37 billion and adjusted earnings of $2.09 per share.\nNvidia is up 22.4% since the beginning of the year, and at $231.14 per share, it is trading close to its 52-week high of $235.74 from May 2026. Investors who bought $1,000 worth of Nvidia's shares 5 years ago would now be looking at an investment worth $11,167.\nALSO WORTH WATCHING: Nvidia's Quiet Partner. Nvidia's chips cost a hundred grand. The connectors that make them work cost even more. One company makes them all.\nEvery AI server needs specialized infrastructure the chip companies don't make. High-speed cables. Power connectors. Thermal sensors. This 90-year-old company built a monopoly on it. The AI boom just started. This stock is still flying under the radar. Claim The Stock Ticker Here for FREE .\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
